def fmt(x,n=3):return f'{x:,.{n}f}'
def percent(x,n=1):return f'{100*x:.{n}f}%'
def link(path,text):return f'<a href="{E(path)}">{E(text)}</a>'
def table(head,body):return '<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+E(s)+'</th>' for s in head)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+str(c)+'</td>' for c in r)+'</tr>' for r in body)+'</tbody></table></div>'
def figure(path,caption):return f'<figure><a href="{E(path)}"><img loading="lazy" src="{E(path)}" alt="{E(caption)}"></a><figcaption>{E(caption)} Click to enlarge.</figcaption></figure>'
sections=[]
def section(id,title,body):sections.append(f'<section id="{id}"><h2>{E(title)}</h2>{body}</section>')

section('start','What we found', '''<p class="lead">The three models share a broad change with depth: typical loop lifetimes get shorter relative to the cloud’s overall distance scale, while late connectivity becomes strongly associated with leading digits. But their best numerical-ordering layers do <strong>not</strong> share one distinctive topological signature.</p>
<div class="cards"><article><span class="tag">Most useful similarity</span><h3>Crystal and StarCoder’s final profiles are close</h3><p>Their final layers are the closest pair of H1 loop-count curves among all 1,152 Crystal–StarCoder layer pairs. This remains a strong similarity after removing total-persistence differences, but not under every diagram comparison.</p></article>
<article><span class="tag">Most useful distinction</span><h3>Best numerical ordering is a different stage</h3><p>Crystal L7 and OpenLLaMA L8 show very little leading-digit component separation, even at their best threshold. Their late layers show much stronger separation. StarCoder is already partly separated at its best-rho layer, L21.</p></article>
<article><span class="tag">Important correction</span><h3>Long H0 bars often describe outliers</h3><p>In every contextual layer, the final merge attaches a group of at most nine targets. It should not be interpreted as two large populations staying separated.</p></article></div>
<p><strong>Reading guide:</strong> H0 describes separate groups and when they connect. H1 describes loops that appear and disappear as the connection distance grows. A loop’s “lifetime” is a range of distances, not elapsed time. Rho measures how well a PCA coordinate orders the numbers. Explained variance measures how much of the cloud’s variation a projection preserves. A Betti curve counts the features present at each distance. Unless stated otherwise, distances are divided by each layer’s typical pair distance so we can compare clouds of different sizes.</p>
<p><strong>Scope:</strong> 94 contextual layers, 10,000 points each, 1,692,308 saved H1 intervals; all 4,371 layer pairs compared with full-support H0/H1 Betti curves. Three embedding levels are treated separately. The previously missing StarCoder embedding H1 was resolved by an exact five-vector duplicate quotient, so all 97 levels now have results available.</p>
<p class="note">These are descriptive findings for these checkpoints, this prompt format and one context per number. They are not proof of a donut, a universal number manifold, statistical significance, or a causal tokenization effect. No new inference or Modal compute was used for this analysis.</p>''')

anchor=[]
for m in models:
 r=rowmap[(m,selections[m]['best_rho_pc1'])];g=h0map[(m,r['layer'])]
 anchor.append([names[m],f'L{r["layer"]} ({percent(r["depth"])})',fmt(r['rho_pc1'],4),percent(r['ev_pc12'],2),f'{r["h1_count"]:,}',fmt(r['h1_max'],4),fmt(r['h1_median'],4),fmt(g['maximum_lead_ari'],4)])
section('rho','1. Do the best-rho layers have the same topology?', '<p><strong>What we checked:</strong> each model’s highest PC1 rank correlation, with its complete H0/H1 diagram and all-scale component membership.</p>'+table(['Model','Selected level','PC1 rho','2D variance','H1 intervals','Longest H1','Median H1','Best leading-digit ARI'],anchor)+'''
<p><strong>What we found:</strong> Crystal and StarCoder have almost identical longest lifetimes (about 0.156), but Crystal has 24,712 intervals versus StarCoder’s 14,239, and nearly twice the typical lifetime. A single maximum would hide that difference. Their H1 Betti-curve distance is 0.06775—larger than about 84% of their cross-model layer-pair distances. OpenLLaMA’s selected layer is different again.</p>
<p>Leading-digit component agreement is very low for Crystal L7 (ARI 0.0331) and OpenLLaMA L8 (0.0252), but substantial for StarCoder L21 (0.7835). All three have high PCA numerical ordering, so there is no single associated connectivity pattern. Their peak depths also differ: 21.9%, 58.3%, and 30.8% through the networks.</p>
<p><strong>Useful?</strong> Yes. This does not support the simple hypothesis that “best rho” automatically selects the same topological regime. It does not rule out shared numerical mechanisms. ARI is chance-adjusted partition agreement: 1 is perfect agreement; about 0 is its random-partition baseline. The reported maximum is selected over distance thresholds, so it is exploratory.</p>'''+figure('figures/anchor_comparisons.png','Selected layers: complete H1 lifetime distributions, H1 Betti curves and H0 Betti curves. Curve panels show normalized distances 0–2; full-support comparisons use every event.'))

ev=[]
for m in models:
 r=rowmap[(m,1)];s=selections[m];p2=rowmap[(m,s['best_rho_pc2'])]
 ev.append([names[m],f'L1 · {percent(r["ev_pc12"],2)}',fmt(r['rho_pc1'],4),f'L{s["best_ev_pc1"]}',f'L{p2["layer"]} · {fmt(p2["rho_pc2"],4)}'])
section('variance','2. Does the best explained variance reveal a common shape?', '<p><strong>What we checked:</strong> highest two-PC variance, highest PC1 variance and highest PC2 rho, keeping these definitions separate.</p>'+table(['Model','Best PC1+PC2 variance','PC1 rho there','Best PC1 variance layer','Best PC2 rho'],ev)+'''
<p><strong>What we found:</strong> all three two-PC variance peaks occur at L1, but their PH differs. OpenLLaMA L1 versus Crystal L1 has an H1 curve distance near the 95th percentile of that model pair; OpenLLaMA versus StarCoder is near the 97th percentile. Higher means less similar. StarCoder L1 has a particularly clear five-component plateau exactly matching digit lengths: 1–9, 10–99, 100–999, 1000–9999 and 10000, over normalized radius 1.05046–1.54418.</p>
<p>Crystal’s highest PC1 variance is instead its final level, L32. Final returned levels include an extra normalization operation, so this peak cannot be attributed solely to the last transformer block. Switching to best PC2 rho selects L9, L9 and L25; their H1 curve distances are 0.06028, 0.06920 and 0.07786, again giving no common signature.</p>
<p><strong>Useful?</strong> Useful for separating projection quality from numerical and topological properties. Explained variance alone is not a good selector for a shared numerical topology.</p>''')

trend=[]
for m in models:
 early=next(x for x in phases if x['model']==m and x['group']=='early');late=next(x for x in phases if x['model']==m and x['group']=='late')
 r=rowmap[(m,selections[m]['final'])]
 trend.append([names[m],fmt(early['h1_median'],5),fmt(late['h1_median'],5),percent(1-late['h1_median']/early['h1_median']),f'{r["h1_count"]:,}',fmt(r['h1_max'],4)])
section('depth','3. What changes with depth?', '<p><strong>What we checked:</strong> every layer, relative-depth anchors, early/middle/late thirds, adjacent changes, and the last three/five layers.</p>'+table(['Model','Early-third typical lifetime','Late-third typical lifetime','Reduction','Final H1 intervals','Final longest lifetime'],trend)+'''
<p>“Typical lifetime” here is the median across layers of each layer’s median H1 lifetime. All lifetimes are divided by that layer’s saved median pair distance.</p>
<p><strong>What we found:</strong> typical lifetimes decline in all three models, and the late stages contain fewer H1 intervals than their early maxima. This is a broad trend, not a monotonic rule for every adjacent layer. The longest normalized H1 lifetime occurs at <strong>L2 in all three models</strong>, rather than at best rho or the final layer. OpenLLaMA’s longest bars rise again in part of the late network even while its median lifetime stays low.</p>
<p>The largest H1 curve changes occur at Crystal L3→L4, StarCoder L1→L2 and OpenLLaMA L2→L3. OpenLLaMA’s L15→L16 change is its second largest. These are descriptive transitions, not statistically inferred change points. Middle and late stages still contain model-specific changes.</p>
<p><strong>Useful?</strong> Yes. There is a shared relative-scale trend, while the timing and exceptional loops differ. Fewer/shorter bars do not on their own mean better numerical ability or a simpler uniquely identified manifold.</p>'''+figure('figures/depth_profiles.png','All 94 contextual layers. Relative depth is layer number divided by the final transformer layer. Black-outlined dots mark best PC1-rho selections.'))

final=[]
for m in models:
 g=h0map[(m,selections[m]['final'])];a=g['all_threshold_maxima']['leading_digit'];details=sorted(a['component_details'],key=lambda x:x['size'],reverse=True)[:9];count=sum(x['size'] for x in details);purity=sum(max(x['leading_digit_counts']) for x in details)/count
 final.append([names[m],fmt(g['maximum_lead_ari'],4),f'{count:,}/10,000',percent(purity,3),fmt(a['threshold_bands']['0.8']['total_width'],5)])
section('groups','4. Are leading-digit groups real connectivity structures?', '<p><strong>What we checked:</strong> every distinct H0 merge threshold using the saved exact minimum spanning trees. These trees recover the connected components of the complete distance graph at every threshold; this is not a calculation on the k=4 graph. Components were formed from distances, then compared to digit labels.</p>'+table(['Final model','Maximum leading-digit ARI','Targets in nine largest components','Leading-digit purity of those cores','Radius width with ARI ≥ 0.8'],final)+'''
<p><strong>What we found:</strong> all three develop leading-digit-associated components. Crystal and StarCoder have broader separation intervals and cover almost all targets in nine pure cores. OpenLLaMA’s separation is weaker and occupies a narrower distance range. All residual small components remain in the analysis.</p>
<p>The first layer to exceed a best-over-radius ARI of 0.8 is Crystal L26/32, StarCoder L22/36 and OpenLLaMA L19/26. The value 0.8 is a descriptive reference, not a significance threshold. The exact best threshold is label-selected; the width shows whether the high agreement is narrow or sustained.</p>
<p><strong>Why this is more useful than color alone:</strong> a perfect numeric k=4 line already has 99.4975% same-leading-digit neighbors. Late hidden neighborhoods also have far larger numeric jumps and many same-leading-digit connections across digit lengths. Final mean numeric gaps are 181.25, 228.10 and 444.42, versus 1.50 for the numeric-line baseline. Together with H0 membership, this supports a digit-associated organization under this prompt, but not a causal tokenizer explanation.</p>
<p><strong>A trap we found:</strong> forcing exactly nine total components gives almost zero digit agreement in every layer. By that late threshold, the main groups have merged and only outliers remain. The nine meaningful large groups occur earlier, alongside residual small components.</p>'''+figure('h0_groups/h0_structure_by_depth.png','Exact H0 connectivity across all thresholds and layers; label agreement is evaluated after distance-based component construction.'))

finaldist=[]
for r in comp['selected_comparisons']:
 if r['selection']=='final':finaldist.append([r['a']+' ↔ '+r['b'],fmt(r['h1'],6),f'{r["h1_cross_pair_percentile"]:.2f}%',fmt(r['h0'],6)])
section('similarity','5. Which models actually look alike topologically?', '<p><strong>What we checked:</strong> 4,371 pairs of layers, using the exact area between full H0/H1 Betti curves; secondary landscape summaries; and selected bottleneck distances with certified bounds. H0 and H1 are kept separate.</p>'+table(['Final-layer pair','H1 curve distance','Percentile within that model pair','H0 curve distance'],finaldist)+'''
<p><strong>What we found:</strong> Crystal L32–StarCoder L36 is the closest H1 curve pair among all 1,152 Crystal–StarCoder combinations. The final-five-layer cross-model median is 0.00801, compared with 0.02494 for Crystal–OpenLLaMA and 0.02641 for StarCoder–OpenLLaMA. Thus this is not solely a one-layer coincidence.</p>
<p>After scaling each H1 curve to unit area, the final Crystal–StarCoder pair remains among the closest 1.56% of their pairs. It therefore shares a similar distance-scale profile, not just low total persistence.</p>
<p><strong>But the answer depends on the feature being compared.</strong> The bottleneck distance focuses on the hardest individual feature to match. For final layers it ranks Crystal–OpenLLaMA closest (0.04594–0.04639), then Crystal–StarCoder (0.06513), then StarCoder–OpenLLaMA (0.07573). So “Crystal and StarCoder have the same topology” would be too strong; “their final H1 count profiles are especially similar” is supported.</p>
<p><strong>A small bottleneck value needs a baseline.</strong> This comparison can discard a feature by matching it to the diagram’s diagonal. Discarding every feature provides a simple upper bound, equal to half the longest lifetime across the two diagrams. At best-rho layers, Crystal–StarCoder reaches 99.89% of that bound and Crystal–OpenLLaMA reaches 100%. At final layers, StarCoder–OpenLLaMA also reaches 100%; Crystal–StarCoder reaches 86.0%, and Crystal–OpenLLaMA 75.73–76.47%. A value near this bound provides little evidence that the strongest loops correspond between models, even if the absolute number looks small.</p>
<p>Depth matching also helps descriptively: cross-model H1 curve-distance median is 0.02905 for depths within five percentage points, versus 0.04235 across all cross-model pairs. Same numbered layers give 0.02881. These differently selected comparison sets do not establish that either alignment is uniquely correct.</p>
<p><strong>Useful?</strong> Yes, especially because the alternative comparisons prevent an overly broad claim. Percentiles here describe this collection of layers; they are not p-values.</p>'''+figure('comparisons/all_layer_similarity.png','Pairwise similarity over all contextual layers. Read the metric labels; a small summary distance does not prove identical shapes or identical numerical labels.'))

section('misleading','6. Which measurements were limited or misleading?', '''<div class="cards"><article><h3>The final H0 merge</h3><p>All 94 final merges attach at most nine points. At final layers, the last detached targets are Crystal 213, StarCoder 1288 and OpenLLaMA 1223. It is an outlier diagnostic here, not a good summary of bulk separation.</p></article>
<article><h3>A “dominant loop” from one long bar</h3><p>The longest bar accounts for less than 0.12% of total H1 lifetime in every layer; the plotted top 30 account for only 0.45–1.98%. This does not invalidate those bars, but screenshots of 30 bars omit nearly all interval mass. The longest/second-longest ratio ranges from 1.0003 to 2.0516, with no consistent isolated-loop pattern.</p></article>
<article><h3>Persistence entropy</h3><p>Normalized entropy is uniformly high, 0.9601–0.9693. It adds little discrimination compared with lifetime distributions, connectivity membership and curve distances. A large rank correlation on this narrow range is not a large practical effect.</p></article></div>
<p>For example, across layers, median H1 lifetime and leading-digit neighbor fraction are strongly anticorrelated in StarCoder (−0.925) and OpenLLaMA (−0.927). But correlations between their adjacent changes are only −0.103 and −0.201. Much of the shared trend can track depth; this is not evidence that one property causes the other. Longest-lifetime versus PC1-rho correlations also differ: Crystal +0.021, StarCoder +0.224, OpenLLaMA −0.608.</p>
<p><strong>Scale matters enormously:</strong> raw longest lifetime increases with depth (rank correlations +0.920, +0.964, +0.975), while normalized longest lifetime decreases (−0.722, −0.476, −0.355). Both statements are true: the clouds expand while loops become smaller relative to typical interpoint distance. “Loops get smaller” without specifying units would be misleading.</p>'''+figure('figures/associations.png','Descriptive associations across dependent layers. First-difference sensitivity and all values are saved in associations.csv; no significance tests or independence assumptions are used.'))

