section('embedding','9. Embedding levels and the resolved missing result', '''<p>Crystal and OpenLLaMA L0 are identical final-“=” token vectors across all targets, so their H0 collapses at zero and they have no H1 loops. These are architectural/extraction baselines, not evidence that the models lack numerical knowledge.</p>
<p><strong>StarCoder L0 is now resolved:</strong> all 10,000 vectors were checked bitwise and contain exactly five distinct vectors, one for each prompt token length. The original duplicate-heavy GPU attempt timed out. A separate exact zero-distance quotient computation gives <strong>zero H1 bars</strong>. Ripser and giotto-ph agree; independent F2 boundary ranks give zero H1 at every threshold; all four positive H0 merge distances equal the archived full-point H0 distances. The original 9,995 zero deaths and one essential component are preserved.</p>
<p>This is an exact reduction of duplicate metric points, not approximate subsampling. It used no cloud job and does not replace the historical failed-run record. The old cloud record remains 96/97; including this validated supplement, all 97 levels are available. Its normalization uses the archived diameter fallback because the sampled median distance is zero.</p>
<p>Its 99.34% two-PC variance describes learned position structure before contextual processing. It should not be selected as evidence of exceptionally strong numerical representation.</p>'''+figure('audit/star_embedding_supplement/persistence.png','Supplementary StarCoder embedding-level PH, computed through exact duplicate reduction and checked against archived H0.')+'<p>'+link('audit/star_embedding_supplement/PROOF_AND_SCOPE.md','Exact reduction proof and scope')+' · '+link('audit/star_embedding_supplement/validation.json','Validation record')+'</p>')

section('limits','10. What this does not settle', '''<p><strong>We cannot label the data “a donut” or “a cup.”</strong> H0/H1 give connectivity and loop information across distance scales. A torus surface has two independent H1 directions and an H2 class; a solid torus is a different object. We did not compute H2. Even matching these counts would not uniquely identify a shape. No H1 would not prove a line either: trees and filled regions can also lack H1.</p>
<p>The H1 figures plot feature birth and death, not number coordinates. Eventual one-component/no-loop behavior is inevitable for a full finite Rips filtration and is not evidence of a number line. A long-lived bar deserves investigation but is not automatically significant; a tiny bar is not automatically meaningless.</p>
<p><strong>Important experimental limits:</strong> the vectors are at the final “=” before answer generation, not isolated numeral embeddings. Each target has a different random context, with only one context per target. Digit count perfectly determines prompt token length and extraction position. Four-digit targets comprise 90% of the cloud; 10000 is the only five-digit target. The next correct output token is the leading digit, so late grouping is compatible with preparing that output. This is a plausible explanation, not a causal test.</p>
<p>Crystal inference used bfloat16; the other models used float32. All stored arrays being float32 does not remove that difference. Final returned layers include LayerNorm/RMSNorm. Architecture, training, dimension, tokenizer and precision vary together; this study does not reproduce the paper’s controlled tokenization experiment.</p>
<p><strong>Not answered by the saved diagrams:</strong> which numbers form a particular H1 loop; whether loops lie within or between digit groups; pointwise local topology; replication under new prompt contexts; comparison to a properly matched geometric null; causal numerical behavior. These require additional experiments or saved cycle/cocycle representatives. Global diagrams cannot be retroactively divided into group-specific H1 diagrams.</p>
<p><strong>Most valuable follow-ups, in order:</strong> repeated and crossed prompt contexts; controlled length/position and alternative next-output tasks; representative cycles/cocycles for selected stable features; matched geometric nulls and perturbation checks; then group/local PH or selected H2 if those specific questions remain motivated. These follow-ups were not run as part of this saved-data analysis.</p>''')

section('methods','11. Methods and reliability', '''<p>The main analysis uses all 10,000 targets at each of the 94 contextual layers. H0 comes from float64 direct-difference Euclidean MSTs. Saved H1 is full-range Vietoris–Rips over F2 with float32 filtration, without kNN restriction or PCA projection. Each layer’s birth/death scale is divided by the saved median of 100,000 seeded off-diagonal pair draws.</p>
<p><strong>H0:</strong> every distinct MST threshold is scanned, including tied edges together. Component-label agreement uses adjusted Rand index. Maxima over radii are exploratory; high-agreement widths and component coverage are reported alongside them.</p>
<p><strong>H1:</strong> all intervals contribute to spectra, entropy, cutoff counts and event-based Betti curves. The infinite H0 bar is excluded from finite-lifetime summaries and cancels between curves. Exact Betti L1 distance integrates the absolute count difference and divides counts by 10,000; it is a pseudometric on diagrams because different interval pairings can share a curve.</p>
<p><strong>Landscapes:</strong> the top five levels are evaluated on a common 1,025-point grid, with all bars competing for those levels. Selected comparisons were checked on 4,097 points; differences were below 0.000005. Higher landscape levels are omitted, so this is not full-landscape equality.</p>
<p><strong>Bottleneck:</strong> selected retained-bar matchings are extended by matching omitted bars to the diagonal, giving a full-diagram upper bound. Nearest-match witnesses against every opposite-diagram bar and triangle bounds give lower bounds. Seven of the nine primary pairs have matching lower/upper values to numerical tolerance; two retain explicitly reported intervals. No truncated result is silently called full exact matching.</p>
<p><strong>Validation:</strong> input artifact hashes, canonical target IDs, scalar summaries, normalized arrays and H0 tree structure were checked. Betti event-tie tests and the equality of lifetime sum to curve area passed. An independent analysis reproduced all 94 rows’ totals, entropy, longest lifetimes, cutoff counts and H0 summaries. Original PH synthetic/backend gates were reviewed. Only 16 of 1,692,308 contextual H1 lifetimes fall at or below the recorded filtration-rounding sensitivity bound; this bound is not a statistical noise threshold and does not cover inference precision or prompt uncertainty.</p>
<p>Associations are descriptive Spearman correlations; adjacent differences provide a sensitivity check to shared depth trends. No p-values are reported, and layers are not treated as independent repeats. Model-pair percentiles and radius-optimized label scores are exploratory. The same-numbered and relative-depth comparison sets differ and should not be treated as a randomized comparison.</p>
<p>Primary references: <a href="https://jmlr.org/papers/v16/bubenik15a.html">Bubenik: persistence landscapes</a>; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.7117">Fasy et al.: confidence sets for persistence diagrams</a>; <a href="https://gudhi.inria.fr/python/latest/bottleneck_distance_user.html">GUDHI: bottleneck distance</a>; <a href="https://ripser.scikit-tda.org/en/latest/notebooks/Representative%20Cocycles.html">Ripser: representative cocycles</a>; <a href="https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf">Hatcher: algebraic topology</a>.</p>''')

